      "id": 2,
      "claim": "If the net‑benefit function is differentiable, the optimal quantity satisfies the first‑order condition: marginal benefit (MB) equals marginal cost (MC).",
      "evidence": "Calculus optimization: the derivative of (TB – TC) with respect to quantity Q is MB – MC; setting this derivative to zero yields MB = MC at the maximum.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Consequently, the consumer will keep eating cookies while MB > MC and will stop when MB = MC for the next cookie, because any further unit would lower net benefit.",
      "evidence": "Marginal analysis: if MB > MC, adding a unit raises net benefit; if MB < MC, adding a unit lowers net benefit.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Option B states exactly this stopping rule: \"the marginal benefit equals the marginal cost of the next cookie.\"",
      "evidence": "Text of option B matches the condition derived in step 3.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore the correct answer is B.",
      "evidence": "Logical deduction from steps 1‑4.",
